OSKit INET Server — Internet Sockets
====================================

Minix 3-style socket server. Fronts the NIC driver (virtio-net)
with an ARP cache + IPv4 stack + UDP + TCP (Reno + SACK) +
ICMP echo. Loopback (127.0.0.1) is served from per-socket queues
without touching the NIC; everything else goes through
`inet_nic_send_packet` / the RX subscription.

Protocol surface
----------------

UDP + raw-ish ops:
- SOCKET (1), BIND (2), SENDTO (3), RECVFROM (4), CLOSE (5)
- RECVFROM_TIMEOUT (18) — used for both timed recv and the
  O_NONBLOCK path (timeout_ms=0 ⇒ immediate EAGAIN via status=1)
- ARP_PROBE (19) — RFC 5227 conflict detection, driven by dhclient
- ICMP_SEND (21) / ICMP_CHECK (22) — ping utility
- SET_IP_CONFIG (16) / GET_IP_CONFIG (17) — driven by dhclient

TCP:
- TCP_CONNECT (10), TCP_SEND (11), TCP_RECV (12), TCP_CLOSE (13),
  TCP_LISTEN (14), TCP_ACCEPT (15)
- TCP_DEBUG (20) — internal state dump for test harness
- TCP_RECV_NB (26) — non-blocking recv returning -EAGAIN (-11)
- TCP_SHUTDOWN (27) — SHUT_WR emits FIN without parking caller
- TCP_GETNAME (25) / UDP_GETNAME (24) — sockaddr_in for
  getsockname / getpeername

Housekeeping:
- PID_EXIT (23) — PM-driven cleanup of sockets whose owner_tid
  matches a terminated thread

All multi-byte integers are big-endian. Each request has a
1-byte command code in `buf[0]`; replies start with a 1-byte
status (0 on success, -errno on failure, -1 for opaque error).
Handlers enforce per-fd ownership: `owner_tid == sender` is
required alongside in-use, so process A can't issue ops on
process B's sockets.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{port_create, port_register, ipc_recv_notify, ipc_reply}
    import oskit.services.{rs_ready, notify_read_self, svc_timer_subscribe, uptime}
    import std.net.{AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, INADDR_ANY, INADDR_LOOPBACK, ipv4, ipv4_octet,
        ETHERTYPE_IPV4, ETHERTYPE_ARP, ARP_OP_REQUEST, ARP_OP_REPLY,
        IP_PROTO_ICMP, IP_PROTO_UDP, IP_PROTO_TCP, ICMP_ECHO_REQ, ICMP_ECHO_REP,
        pkt_read_u16, pkt_match_ipv4}


Protocol command codes (byte value of buf[0]).

    val INET_CMD_SOCKET       = 1
    val INET_CMD_BIND         = 2
    val INET_CMD_SENDTO       = 3
    val INET_CMD_RECVFROM     = 4
    val INET_CMD_CLOSE        = 5
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT  = 10
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SEND     = 11
    val INET_CMD_TCP_RECV     = 12
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE    = 13
    val INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN   = 14
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT   = 15
    val INET_CMD_SET_IP_CONFIG = 16
    val INET_CMD_GET_IP_CONFIG = 17
    val INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT = 18
    val INET_CMD_ARP_PROBE    = 19
    val INET_CMD_TCP_DEBUG    = 20
    val INET_CMD_ICMP_SEND    = 21
    val INET_CMD_ICMP_CHECK   = 22
    val INET_CMD_PID_EXIT     = 23
    val INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME  = 24
    val INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME  = 25
    val INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB  = 26
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN = 27
    val INET_CMD_POLL         = 28
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB    = 29
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB  = 30
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE = 31
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB = 32
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB = 33
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF   = 34
    val INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF   = 35
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE = 36
    val INET_CMD_TCP_OOO_SELFTEST = 99


Sub-ops for INET_CMD_ARP_PROBE. A single command code fans out
into three operations so RFC 5227 conflict detection can be
driven from userspace:

- `START` arms the probe state with a target IP.
- `SEND` transmits one ARP probe (sender IP = 0.0.0.0).
- `CHECK` returns whether any defending ARP was observed and
  clears the probe state.

    val ARP_PROBE_OP_START    = 0
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_SEND     = 1
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_CHECK    = 2
    val ARP_PROBE_OP_ANNOUNCE = 3


Capacity limits.

    val INET_MAX_SOCKETS = 32
    val INET_MAX_QUEUE   = 4
    val INET_MAX_DATA    = 512
    val INET_MSG_BUF     = 540   // cmd(1)+fd(1)+hdr(9)+maxlen(2)+data(512)+slop


A queued datagram awaiting recvfrom. Source address is stored
host-order to simplify comparisons.

    struct InetDgram
        src_ip: u32
        src_port: int
        data: [512]byte
        data_len: int


Per-socket state. `bound_port == 0` means unbound (port 0 is
reserved for auto-bind, which isn't implemented yet).

    struct InetSocket
        in_use: int
        owner_tid: int
        bound: int
        bound_ip: u32
        bound_port: int
        queue: [4]InetDgram
        q_head: int
        q_tail: int
        q_count: int
        pending_tid: int
        pending_maxlen: int
        recv_deadline: int

    var inet_sockets: [32]InetSocket
    var inet_port = -1


RFC 5227 ARP probe state. `arp_probe_target` is the IPv4
address currently being probed (0 = no probe active).
`arp_probe_seen` is set by the ARP RX path when any ARP
request OR reply arrives whose sender IP matches
`arp_probe_target` and whose sender MAC is not our own (NIC
loopback guard per §2.1.1). The dhclient-side probe loop
clears both fields via the CHECK op when the probe window
ends.

    var arp_probe_target: u32 = u32(0)
    var arp_probe_seen:   int = 0


Returns 1 if `frame` is an ARP packet whose sender IP matches
the probe target AND whose sender MAC is not our own. Returns
0 otherwise (including when no probe is active, when the
target doesn't match, or when the frame came from our own MAC
via NIC loopback). Used by the ARP RX path to flag defenders.

    inet_arp_probe_matches(frame: *byte) -> int
        if arp_probe_target == u32(0)
            return 0
        val sender_ip = ipv4(
            int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff,
            int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff)
        if sender_ip != arp_probe_target
            return 0
        // Sender MAC lives at frame+14+8..13 (ARP SHA field).
        // Compare against our own MAC to filter out NIC loopback.
        var i = 0
        var same = 1
        while i < 6
            if frame[14 + 8 + i] != inet_our_mac[i]
                same = 0
            i += 1
        if same != 0
            return 0
        1


### Byte-buffer helpers

Read / write the big-endian u16s used in the wire protocol. No
alignment assumptions — pointers come from IPC buffers.

    inet_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)

    inet_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)

    inet_read_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int) -> u32
        ipv4(int(p[off]), int(p[off + 1]), int(p[off + 2]), int(p[off + 3]))

    inet_write_ipv4(p: *byte, off: int, ip: u32)
        p[off]     = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 0))
        p[off + 1] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 1))
        p[off + 2] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 2))
        p[off + 3] = byte(ipv4_octet(ip, 3))


Initialize the socket table once. Called from the server main
loop before `port_register` so sockets are consistent before any
client can reach us.

    inet_init_sockets()
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            inet_sockets[i].in_use = 0
            inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
            i += 1
        // Note: `inet_udp_fire_seq` (in inet_proto.lsysl) is left at
        // its default-zeroed state. The shim re-syncs `last_seen_seq`
        // on every EPOLL_CTL_ADD anyway, so a recycled slot's stale
        // counter is harmless — first scan treats it as the live
        // baseline.


### Locate a bound socket

Linear scan — fine at 32 sockets; a hash on (ip, port) would be
the obvious upgrade once the stack grows.

    inet_find_bound(ip: u32, port: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].bound != 0
                    if inet_sockets[i].bound_port == port
                        val bi = inet_sockets[i].bound_ip
                        if bi == INADDR_ANY
                            return i
                        if bi == ip
                            return i
            i += 1
        -1


### Allocate an ephemeral source port

Linear scan from 49152 upward (RFC 6335 suggested range), returning
the first port no existing socket is bound to. Returns -1 if the
entire range is occupied — won't happen in practice with 32 sockets
but the guard keeps the loop bounded.

    inet_alloc_ephemeral_port() -> int
        var port = 49152
        while port < 65536
            if inet_find_bound_port(port) < 0
                return port
            port += 1
        -1


### Locate any socket bound to `port`

Port-only variant for UDP RX dispatch. The guest has a single unicast
IPv4 address plus `INADDR_ANY`, so IP matching on inbound packets
would just restate what's already true (the nic delivered the frame
to our MAC). Returning the first port match keeps the RX path simple
and matches how typical single-homed stacks behave.

    inet_find_bound_port(port: int) -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].bound != 0
                    if inet_sockets[i].bound_port == port
                        return i
            i += 1
        -1


### Enqueue an incoming datagram

Returns 1 on success, 0 if the queue is full (caller drops the
packet per UDP semantics).

    inet_enqueue(fd: int, src_ip: u32, src_port: int, data: *byte, dlen: int) -> int
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count >= INET_MAX_QUEUE
            return 0
        val slot = inet_sockets[fd].q_tail
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_ip = src_ip
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_port = src_port
        var copy_len = dlen
        if copy_len > INET_MAX_DATA
            copy_len = INET_MAX_DATA
        var i = 0
        while i < copy_len
            inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data[i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data_len = copy_len
        inet_sockets[fd].q_tail = (slot + 1) % INET_MAX_QUEUE
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count += 1
        inet_epoll_fire(1, fd)
        1


### Deliver a datagram directly to a socket's pending recvfrom

Used by both the loopback sendto path and the UDP RX path. Assumes
`inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0`. Clears the waiter slot, builds
the recvfrom reply (same shape as `inet_deliver_head` produces), and
replies via IPC.

    inet_deliver_to_waiter(fd: int, src_ip: u32, src_port: int, data: *byte, dlen: int)
        val waiter = inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid
        val mlen = inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = -1
        inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = 0
        var wr: [540]byte
        var deliver_len = dlen
        if deliver_len > mlen
            deliver_len = mlen
        wr[0] = 0
        wr[1] = byte(AF_INET & 0xff)
        inet_write_u16(wr, 2, src_port)
        inet_write_ipv4(wr, 4, src_ip)
        inet_write_u16(wr, 8, deliver_len)
        var i = 0
        while i < deliver_len
            wr[10 + i] = data[i]
            i += 1
        ipc_reply(waiter, wr, 10 + deliver_len)


### Deliver one queued datagram to a pending recvfrom

Writes the recvfrom reply into `reply` and returns its length.
Only called when we know there's both a queued dgram and a
waiter. `maxlen` caps how many payload bytes we hand back.

    inet_deliver_head(fd: int, maxlen: int, reply: *byte) -> int
        val slot = inet_sockets[fd].q_head
        val src_ip = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_ip
        val src_port = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].src_port
        var dlen = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data_len
        if dlen > maxlen
            dlen = maxlen
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(AF_INET & 0xff)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 2, src_port)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 4, src_ip)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 8, dlen)
        var i = 0
        while i < dlen
            reply[10 + i] = inet_sockets[fd].queue[slot].data[i]
            i += 1
        inet_sockets[fd].q_head = (slot + 1) % INET_MAX_QUEUE
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count -= 1
        10 + dlen


### SOCKET handler

    inet_handle_socket(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val family = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val stype  = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        if stype != SOCK_DGRAM
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        var idx = -1
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use == 0
                idx = i
                i = INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            else
                i += 1
        if idx < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        inet_sockets[idx].in_use = 1
        inet_sockets[idx].owner_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[idx].bound = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].bound_ip = INADDR_ANY
        inet_sockets[idx].bound_port = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_head = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_tail = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].q_count = 0
        inet_sockets[idx].pending_tid = -1
        inet_sockets[idx].recv_deadline = 0
        // No need to reset inet_udp_fire_seq[idx] — see
        // `inet_init_sockets` for why a stale counter is harmless.
        var reply: [2]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(idx & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


Validate an fd belongs to `sender`. Rejects out-of-range fds,
free slots, and slots whose `owner_tid` points at a different
thread — the latter is the ownership check that prevents process
A from issuing IPC with process B's inet fd (A would need to
guess the fd number today, but the guard makes the surface
explicit regardless). Returns 1 if OK, 0 otherwise. Call sites
pass the IPC `sender` argument.

    inet_fd_valid(fd: int, sender: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return 0
        if fd >= INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            return 0
        if inet_sockets[fd].in_use == 0
            return 0
        if inet_sockets[fd].owner_tid != sender
            return 0
        1


### BIND handler

    inet_handle_bind(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val family = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        if port <= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if ip != INADDR_ANY
            if ip != INADDR_LOOPBACK
                var err: [1]byte
                err[0] = byte(-1)
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
                return
        if inet_find_bound(ip, port) >= 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        inet_sockets[fd].bound = 1
        inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip = ip
        inet_sockets[fd].bound_port = port
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### SENDTO handler

Loopback (`INADDR_LOOPBACK`) is delivered in-process against the
socket table. Non-loopback destinations go out the wire via
`inet_send_udp` — requires the socket to be bound so we have a valid
source port, and requires the gateway MAC to have been learned via
boot ARP. Either failure reports `status=-1, nsent=0`; UDP semantics
let us drop unmatched loopback targets silently with
`status=0, nsent=dlen`.

    inet_handle_sendto(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val family = int(buf[2]) & 0xff
        if family != AF_INET
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val dst_port = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val dst_ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        val dlen = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val data: *byte = buf + 11

Auto-bind unbound sockets to an ephemeral src port (matches POSIX
`sendto(2)` semantics). Covers both loopback and wire paths so
receivers always see a non-zero `src_port` in `recvfrom` replies.
Fails sendto only if the ephemeral range is exhausted — 32 sockets
vs. 16K ports, so not in practice.

        if inet_sockets[fd].bound == 0
            val eph = inet_alloc_ephemeral_port()
            if eph < 0
                var err: [3]byte
                err[0] = byte(-1)
                err[1] = 0
                err[2] = 0
                ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
                return
            inet_sockets[fd].bound = 1
            inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip = INADDR_ANY
            inet_sockets[fd].bound_port = eph
        val src_port = inet_sockets[fd].bound_port
        if dst_ip == INADDR_LOOPBACK
            val target = inet_find_bound(dst_ip, dst_port)
            if target >= 0
                if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
                    inet_deliver_to_waiter(target, INADDR_LOOPBACK, src_port, data, dlen)
                else
                    inet_enqueue(target, INADDR_LOOPBACK, src_port, data, dlen)
            var reply: [3]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, dlen)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        val r = inet_send_udp(dst_ip, dst_port, src_port, data, dlen)
        if r < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            err[2] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, dlen)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)


### RECVFROM handler

If the socket already has queued datagrams, deliver the head
immediately. Otherwise park the sender's thread id in
`pending_tid` and return without replying — the reply is
produced later when a matching sendto arrives.

Only one pending recv per socket. A second recv while one is
already pending is rejected. Good enough for single-threaded
programs; multi-waiter support is on the list.

    inet_handle_recvfrom(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 2)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > INET_MAX_DATA
            maxlen = INET_MAX_DATA
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
            var reply: [540]byte
            val n = inet_deliver_head(fd, maxlen, reply)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, n)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen = maxlen
        // No reply — deferred until a dgram arrives or close.


### RECVFROM_TIMEOUT handler

Same as RECVFROM but with a bounded wait. Request layout:
`cmd(1) + fd(1) + maxlen(2) + timeout_ms(4)` = 8 bytes. The
reply shape matches RECVFROM so callers can use the same parser.

Status byte in the reply distinguishes outcomes:
- `0` — data delivered
- `1` — timed out (no datagram within `timeout_ms`)
- `-1` — error

`inet_udp_scan_timeouts()` in the server main loop replies with
status=1 when `now >= recv_deadline`. The deadline is expressed
in scheduler ticks (100 Hz → 10 ms each). We round timeout up so
a `1 ms` request still fires on the next tick rather than
possibly firing immediately.

    inet_handle_recvfrom_timeout(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 2)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > INET_MAX_DATA
            maxlen = INET_MAX_DATA
        val tms_hi = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        val tms_lo = inet_read_u16(buf, 6)
        val timeout_ms = (tms_hi << 16) | tms_lo
        if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
            var reply: [540]byte
            val n = inet_deliver_head(fd, maxlen, reply)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, n)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            var err: [10]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 10)
            return
        if timeout_ms <= 0
            var tr: [10]byte
            tr[0] = byte(1)
            ipc_reply(sender, tr, 10)
            return
        val ticks = (timeout_ms + 9) / 10
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = sender
        inet_sockets[fd].pending_maxlen = maxlen
        inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = int(uptime()) + ticks
        // No reply — deferred until a dgram arrives, timeout
        // expires, or close.


### TCP_DEBUG handler

Expose internal congestion-control state for a TCP socket so
the test harness can assert cwnd / ssthresh / recovery-state
behaviour without having to reverse-engineer it from timing.
Read-only — no state is mutated.

```
request: cmd(1) + fd(2)                 = 3 bytes
reply:   status(1) + state(1)
       + cwnd(4)          + ssthresh(4)
       + snd_una(4)       + snd_nxt(4)
       + snd_wnd(4)       + send_len(4)
       + fast_recovery(4) + dup_ack_count(4)
       + sack_ok(4)       + rexmit_count(4)     = 42 bytes
```

    inet_handle_tcp_debug(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [42]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 42)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(tcp_sockets[fd].state & 0xff)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 2,  tcp_sockets[fd].snd_cwnd)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 6,  tcp_sockets[fd].snd_ssthresh)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 10, int(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_una))
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 14, int(tcp_sockets[fd].snd_nxt))
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 18, tcp_sockets[fd].snd_wnd)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 22, tcp_sockets[fd].send_len)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 26, tcp_sockets[fd].fast_recovery)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 30, tcp_sockets[fd].dup_ack_count)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 34, tcp_sockets[fd].sack_ok)
        inet_dbg_write_i32(reply, 38, tcp_sockets[fd].rexmit_count)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 42)

    inet_dbg_write_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >>  8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte( v        & 0xff)


### ARP probe handler

Four-operation command driven by dhclient for RFC 5227
conflict detection.

```
request: cmd(1) + op(1) + target_ip(4)   = 6 bytes
reply:   status(1) + seen(1)             = 2 bytes
```

`seen` is meaningful only for `CHECK` and is 1 if any
defender was observed since the preceding `START`.

    inet_handle_arp_probe(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val op = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val target_ip = ipv4(
            int(buf[2]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[3]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[4]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[5]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [2]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = 0
        if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_START
            arp_probe_target = target_ip
            arp_probe_seen = 0
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_SEND
            val rc = inet_send_arp_probe(
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 0),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 1),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 2),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 3))
            if rc < 0
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_CHECK
            reply[1] = byte(arp_probe_seen & 0xff)
            arp_probe_target = u32(0)
            arp_probe_seen = 0
        else if op == ARP_PROBE_OP_ANNOUNCE
            val arc = inet_send_arp_announce(
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 0),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 1),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 2),
                ipv4_octet(target_ip, 3))
            if arc < 0
                reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)


### ICMP echo send / check

`ping`-style: the client registers a ping (id, seq) and asks us
to emit an ECHO_REQUEST to `dst_ip`; later it polls for a
matching ECHO_REPLY. Both operations are non-blocking: SEND
returns immediately after transmit, CHECK returns "still
waiting" if no reply has arrived yet. ARP misses are surfaced
to the caller so it can retry.

```
SEND request:  cmd(1) + dst_ip(4) + id(2) + seq(2)   = 9 bytes
SEND reply:    status(1)                             = 1 byte
CHECK request: cmd(1) + id(2) + seq(2)               = 5 bytes
CHECK reply:   status(1) + src_ip(4) + rtt(4)        = 9 bytes
    status: 0 = reply arrived, 1 = still waiting, -1 = no slot
```

    inet_handle_icmp_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val dst_ip = ipv4(
            int(buf[1]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[2]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[3]) & 0xff,
            int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        val id  = (int(buf[5]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[6]) & 0xff)
        val seq = (int(buf[7]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[8]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        if inet_ping_register(id, seq, int(uptime())) < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if inet_send_icmp_echo_to(dst_ip, id, seq) < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


    inet_handle_icmp_check(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val id  = (int(buf[1]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[2]) & 0xff)
        val seq = (int(buf[3]) & 0xff) << 8 | (int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        var reply: [9]byte
        var k = 0
        while k < 9
            reply[k] = 0
            k += 1
        var src_ip: u32 = u32(0)
        var rtt: int = 0
        val rc = inet_ping_check(id, seq, &src_ip, &rtt)
        if rc < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
        else if rc == 0
            reply[0] = 1
        else
            reply[0] = 0
            reply[1] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 0))
            reply[2] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 1))
            reply[3] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 2))
            reply[4] = byte(ipv4_octet(src_ip, 3))
            reply[5] = byte((rtt >> 24) & 0xff)
            reply[6] = byte((rtt >> 16) & 0xff)
            reply[7] = byte((rtt >> 8) & 0xff)
            reply[8] = byte(rtt & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 9)


### UDP recv-timeout scanner

Called from the inet main loop on every wake. Scans every
socket with a pending recvfrom that has a non-zero deadline, and
when the deadline has passed replies with status=1 (timeout). We
clear the waiter before replying so a datagram arriving on the
same tick doesn't wake the thread twice.

Timer wakes happen every ~50 ms (`svc_timer_subscribe(5)` in
inet_server), so worst-case overshoot on an idle port is one
tick period. Good enough for DHCP-scale retransmits.

    inet_udp_scan_timeouts()
        val now = int(uptime())
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].pending_tid >= 0
                    val dl = inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline
                    if dl != 0
                        if now >= dl
                            val waiter = inet_sockets[i].pending_tid
                            inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
                            inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
                            var tr: [10]byte
                            tr[0] = byte(1)
                            ipc_reply(waiter, tr, 10)
            i += 1


### CLOSE handler

Closes the slot. If a recv was pending, reply with an error so
the caller doesn't block forever.

    inet_handle_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        if inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid >= 0
            val waiter = inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid
            inet_sockets[fd].pending_tid = -1
            inet_sockets[fd].recv_deadline = 0
            var wr: [10]byte
            wr[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(waiter, wr, 10)
        inet_sockets[fd].in_use = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].bound = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_count = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_head = 0
        inet_sockets[fd].q_tail = 0
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### TCP command handlers

All four commands are blocking: they do NOT reply before the
wakeup condition is met. Handlers stash the caller tid in the
per-socket waiter slot (connect_waiter / recv_waiter / close_waiter)
and the state machine / timer scan delivers the reply later.
SEND is the one exception — POSIX `write()` semantics mean it
replies as soon as bytes land in the send buffer.

Wire layout (all big-endian u16):
- TCP_CONNECT: cmd(1), ip(4), port(2) -> status(1)
- TCP_SEND: cmd(1), fd(2), len(2), data(len) -> status(1), written(2)
- TCP_RECV: cmd(1), fd(2), maxlen(2) -> status(1), len(2), data(len); len=0 means EOF
- TCP_CLOSE: cmd(1), fd(2) -> status(1)

`fd` is a 16-bit raw socket index. First byte at offset 1 is the
high byte (0), second (offset 2) is the low byte / index.

    inet_read_u16_at(buf: *byte, off: int) -> int
        inet_read_u16(buf, off)

    inet_handle_tcp_connect(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, sender, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        if s < 0
            var err: [2]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            err[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 2)
            return
        // Deferred reply — inet_tcp_state_step replies when ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking connect. Allocate slot + emit SYN like the
blocking variant, but pass `-1` for `caller_tid` so the slot's
connect_waiter is the unset sentinel and the SYN-ACK path
won't try to deliver an IPC reply. Reply immediately with the
slot fd; the shim translates that to `-EINPROGRESS` for the
user. EPOLLOUT delivery once ESTABLISHED comes through the
existing `inet_epoll_fire(2, s)` at SYN_SENT → ESTABLISHED.

    inet_handle_tcp_connect_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 9)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_connect(sender, -1, ip, port, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_send(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val dlen = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        val data: *byte = buf + 5
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        val n = inet_tcp_do_send(fd, data, dlen)
        if n < 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        var reply: [3]byte
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, n)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)

    inet_handle_tcp_recv(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
        tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when data arrives.

Non-blocking TCP recv. Same wire format as TCP_RECV (cmd + fd +
maxlen). Three reply shapes:
  - data ready → status=0 + len + data (same as TCP_RECV).
  - peer FIN  → status=0 + len=0 (EOF, same as TCP_RECV).
  - no data   → status=-11 (-EAGAIN, 3 bytes).
Invalid fd / closed socket still produces the -1 error reply.
Unlike TCP_RECV this never parks the caller; the shim picks
the NB variant when `O_NONBLOCK` is set on the fd.

    inet_handle_tcp_recv_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val maxlen_raw = inet_read_u16(buf, 3)
        var maxlen = maxlen_raw
        if maxlen > 500
            maxlen = 500
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [3]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(err, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 3)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            var give = tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len
            if give > maxlen
                give = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, give)
            return
        if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_waiter = sender
            tcp_sockets[fd].recv_maxlen = maxlen
            inet_tcp_reply_recv(fd, 0)
            return
        var eagain: [3]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        inet_write_u16(eagain, 1, 0)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 3)

Non-consuming readiness query for the shim's epoll
implementation. Caller passes (kind, fd) and gets back a
`ready_mask` packed into the reply: bit 0 = readable, bit 2
= writable, bit 3 = error, bit 4 = hangup. Same bit positions
as Linux's `EPOLLIN` / `EPOLLOUT` / `EPOLLERR` / `EPOLLHUP`.

Wire: [cmd(1), kind(1), fd(u16)] = 4 bytes.
Reply: [status(1), mask(u16), fire_seq(u32 le)] = 7 bytes.

`fire_seq` is the per-fd monotonic counter that
`inet_epoll_fire` bumps on every readiness edge — the shim
caches it per `epoll_table` entry and clears that entry's
`last_reported` only when the seq advances, so EPOLLET
subscribers don't over-fire on unrelated fds in the same
instance.

kind: 1 = UDP, 2 = TCP (listen sockets are detected by state).

For UDP, readable iff there's a queued datagram; writable
always. For TCP regular sockets, readable iff recv_len > 0 or
peer_fin (so an EOF read won't block); writable iff there is
send-buffer space and we're connected; hangup iff peer_fin.
For TCP listen sockets, readable iff the accept queue has a
ready child.

    inet_handle_poll(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        var mask = 0
        var seq = 0
        if kind == 1
            // UDP — owner check via inet_fd_valid which scopes
            // by sender PTBR/tid.
            if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) != 0
                if inet_sockets[fd].q_count > 0
                    mask = mask | 0x1
                mask = mask | 0x4
                seq = inet_udp_fire_seq[fd]
        else if kind == 2
            if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) != 0
                val st = tcp_sockets[fd].state
                if st == TCP_STATE_LISTEN
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].accept_head != tcp_sockets[fd].accept_tail
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                else
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].recv_len > 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                    if tcp_sockets[fd].peer_fin != 0
                        mask = mask | 0x1
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                    if st == TCP_STATE_ESTABLISHED || st == TCP_STATE_CLOSE_WAIT
                        if tcp_sockets[fd].send_len < tcp_sockets[fd].send_buf_size
                            mask = mask | 0x4
                    if st == TCP_STATE_CLOSED
                        mask = mask | 0x8
                        mask = mask | 0x10
                seq = tcp_sockets[fd].fire_seq
        var reply: [7]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, mask)
        // fire_seq little-endian (matches the shim's epoll-event
        // serialization throughout `oskit.posix.shim`).
        reply[3] = byte(seq & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte((seq >> 8) & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((seq >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((seq >> 24) & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)

shutdown(fd, how). `how` values: 0 = SHUT_RD, 1 = SHUT_WR,
2 = SHUT_RDWR. SHUT_RD is a no-op today — we don't track a
separate "read shut down" state, so the peer sees no effect and
subsequent recvs still deliver data. SHUT_WR emits our FIN via
`inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr` without parking the caller. SHUT_RDWR
is treated as SHUT_WR (a proper SHUT_RDWR would also drop
buffered recv; deferred). Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16) + how(u8) = 4.
Reply: status(1).

    inet_handle_tcp_shutdown(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val how = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if how != 0
            val _ = inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        val r = inet_tcp_do_close(fd, sender)
        if r == 1
            var reply: [1]byte
            reply[0] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        if r < 0
            var err: [1]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 1)
            return
        // Deferred reply — state step delivers when FIN is acked.

SO_LINGER `l_linger = 0` close path.  Shim sends this when the
caller has set linger-on with a zero timeout: emit RST, free
slot, no FIN, no TIME_WAIT.  Reply is immediate (1-byte status)
since no FIN handshake happens.

    inet_handle_tcp_abort_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_abort(fd)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_listen(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val port = inet_read_u16(buf, 1)
        val backlog = inet_read_u16(buf, 3) & 0xffff
        val sndbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 5)
        val rcvbuf = inet_read_u16(buf, 7)
        val s = inet_tcp_begin_listen(sender, port, backlog, sndbuf, rcvbuf)
        var reply: [2]byte
        if s < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            reply[1] = 0
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        reply[1] = byte(s & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 2)

    inet_handle_tcp_accept(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_do_accept(sender, fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // child == -1 → caller parked; state_step will reply once a
        // SYN_RCVD child reaches ESTABLISHED.

Non-blocking accept. Identical wire to `INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT` but
never parks the caller — replies -EAGAIN if the queue is empty
so an event-loop client can drive accept() entirely from epoll
notifications. The shim picks this variant when the listen fd
has `O_NONBLOCK` set.

    inet_handle_tcp_accept_nb(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        val child = inet_tcp_try_accept(fd)
        if child == -2
            var err: [8]byte
            err[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, err, 8)
            return
        if child >= 0
            inet_tcp_reply_accept(sender, 0, child)
            return
        // Queue empty — return -EAGAIN without parking.
        var eagain: [8]byte
        eagain[0] = byte(-11)
        ipc_reply(sender, eagain, 8)


### inet_handle_set_ip_config

Install a new (ip, mask, gw) triple via `inet_set_ip_config`. Used
by the DHCP client once a lease is bound, and exposed as an IPC op
so any userspace tool (ifconfig, tests) can drive it. Wire format:
cmd(1) + ip(4) + mask(4) + gw(4) = 13 bytes in; 1 byte status out.

    inet_handle_set_ip_config(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val ip = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 1)
        val mask = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 5)
        val gw = inet_read_ipv4(buf, 9)
        inet_set_ip_config(ip, mask, gw)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### inet_handle_pid_exit

Reclaim every socket owned by a terminated thread. PM sends the
dead process's main_tid as a big-endian i32 at buf[1..5]; inet
scans both the UDP and TCP tables and closes any socket whose
`owner_tid` matches. This is how we prevent inet_sockets[] /
tcp_sockets[] slots from leaking when a process exits without
calling close(). Wire format: cmd(1) + tid(4) = 5 in, status(1)
out. Cleanup is ungraceful — TCP sockets skip FIN and go straight
to CLOSED, which is fine for the pid-exit case (the process is
gone and the peer will observe connection loss via timeout or a
future RST).

    inet_handle_pid_exit(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val tid = ((int(buf[1]) & 0xff) << 24) | ((int(buf[2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(buf[3]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(buf[4]) & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_MAX_SOCKETS
            if inet_sockets[i].in_use != 0
                if inet_sockets[i].owner_tid == tid
                    inet_sockets[i].in_use = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].bound = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_count = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_head = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].q_tail = 0
                    inet_sockets[i].pending_tid = -1
                    inet_sockets[i].recv_deadline = 0
            i += 1
        inet_tcp_cleanup_owner(tid)
        inet_epoll_drop_owner(tid)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### inet_handle_udp_getname

Return the local endpoint of a UDP socket: (port, ip). Used by
`getsockname` from the POSIX shim. UDP has no persistent peer, so
`getpeername` on UDP short-circuits in the shim and this command
never fires with a "peer" variant — hence a single op with no
which-flag. Wire: cmd(1) + fd(u8) = 2 bytes in. Reply: status(1)
+ port(2 BE) + ip(4) = 7 bytes. An unbound socket reports
`0.0.0.0:0`.

    inet_handle_udp_getname(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        var reply: [7]byte
        if inet_fd_valid(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        if inet_sockets[fd].bound != 0
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, inet_sockets[fd].bound_port)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, inet_sockets[fd].bound_ip)
        else
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### inet_handle_tcp_getname

Return either the local or peer endpoint of a TCP socket. Wire:
cmd(1) + fd(u16) + which(u8) = 4 bytes in; which == 0 means
local, which == 1 means peer. Reply: status(1) + port(2 BE) +
ip(4) = 7 bytes. Returns `-ENOTCONN` (-107) for a peer query on
a socket that hasn't reached ESTABLISHED. The heavy lifting lives
in `inet_tcp_getname` (inet_proto.lsysl) because it touches
`tcp_sockets[]` state.

    inet_handle_tcp_getname(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val which = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [7]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, u32(0))
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)
            return
        var port = 0
        var ip = u32(0)
        val status = inet_tcp_getname(fd, which, &port, &ip)
        reply[0] = byte(status)
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, port)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 3, ip)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 7)


### inet_handle_tcp_setbuf / inet_handle_tcp_getbuf

setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF) / setsockopt(SO_RCVBUF). The shim translates
the user `int optval` into a 16-bit BE size on the wire (clamped on
the inet side to [TCP_BUF_MIN, TCP_BUF_MAX]). `kind == 0` selects
SNDBUF, `kind == 1` selects RCVBUF.

SET wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) + size(u16 BE) = 6 bytes in;
reply is 1 byte status (0 = ok, -1 = bad fd / bad kind).

GET wire: cmd(1) + fd(u16 BE) + kind(1) = 4 bytes in; reply is
1 byte status + u16 BE size = 3 bytes out (size 0 on error).

    inet_handle_tcp_setbuf(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        val size = inet_read_u16(buf, 4)
        var reply: [1]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val rc = inet_tcp_resize_buf(fd, kind, size)
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_tcp_getbuf(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 1) & 0xffff
        val kind = int(buf[3]) & 0xff
        var reply: [3]byte
        if inet_tcp_slot_owned(fd, sender) == 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        val sz = inet_tcp_get_bufsize(fd, kind)
        if sz < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-1)
            inet_write_u16(reply, 1, 0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)
            return
        reply[0] = 0
        inet_write_u16(reply, 1, sz)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 3)


### inet_handle_get_ip_config

Return the current (ip, mask, gw). Reply: status(1) + ip(4) +
mask(4) + gw(4) = 13 bytes. Payload-less request (just cmd).

    inet_handle_get_ip_config(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        var reply: [13]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 1, inet_get_our_ip())
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 5, inet_get_mask())
        inet_write_ipv4(reply, 9, inet_get_gw())
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 13)


Test-only: drive inet_tcp_state_step through a deliberately out-of-
order segment sequence against a scratch socket and verify the
reorder queue yields the right rcv_nxt / recv_buf contents. Reply
is 1 byte: 0 on pass, nonzero error code on failure.

    inet_handle_tcp_ooo_selftest(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val rc = inet_tcp_ooo_selftest()
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(rc & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### UDP — inbound

Dispatch an IPv4/UDP frame that already passed the destination-IP
check. Pulls header fields, looks up a socket bound to `dst_port`,
and either hands the datagram to a waiting recvfrom or enqueues it.
Dropping on no-match matches standard UDP semantics.

    inet_handle_udp(frame: *byte, total_len: int, ihl_bytes: int)
        val udp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
        if total_len < udp_off + 8
            return
        val src_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off)
        val dst_port = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 2)
        val udp_len = pkt_read_u16(frame, udp_off + 4)
        if udp_len < 8
            return
        if total_len < udp_off + udp_len
            return
        val payload_len = udp_len - 8
        val data: *byte = frame + udp_off + 8
        val src_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
        // Read dst from the wire rather than hardcoding our own IP:
        // broadcast UDP (DHCP OFFER/ACK) arrives with dst=255.255.255.255
        // and the checksum must pseudo-header it accordingly.
        val dst_ip = ipv4(int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 18]) & 0xff, int(frame[14 + 19]) & 0xff)
        if inet_udp_verify(src_ip, dst_ip, frame, udp_off, udp_len) == 0
            return
        val target = inet_find_bound_port(dst_port)
        if target < 0
            return
        if inet_sockets[target].pending_tid >= 0
            inet_deliver_to_waiter(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)
        else
            inet_enqueue(target, src_ip, src_port, data, payload_len)


### EtherType dispatch

Called by the RX drain for every frame. ARP requests for us ->
reply, ARP reply from gateway -> cache gw MAC + send one ICMP
echo, ICMP echo request -> reply, ICMP echo reply -> diag count,
UDP -> socket dispatch. Returns 1 if we acted, 0 otherwise
(diagnostic only).

    inet_handle_frame(frame: *byte, len: int) -> int
        if len < 14
            return 0
        val etype = pkt_read_u16(frame, 12)
        if etype == ETHERTYPE_ARP
            if len < 14 + 28
                return 0
            val op = pkt_read_u16(frame, 14 + 6)
            // ARP probe conflict detection (RFC 5227 §2.1.1).
            // Every ARP frame whose sender IP matches the address
            // being probed counts as a defender — *unless* its
            // sender MAC matches ours, in which case it's our own
            // probe looped back through the NIC. We watch both
            // REQUEST and REPLY since a defender can use either.
            if inet_arp_probe_matches(frame) != 0
                arp_probe_seen = 1
            if op == ARP_OP_REQUEST
                if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 24, INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D) == 0
                    return 0
                inet_send_arp_reply(frame)
                return 1
            if op == ARP_OP_REPLY
                // Cache (sender IP, sender MAC) for any reply — the
                // payer stack wants to know anyone who answered, not
                // just the gateway. ARP frame layout:
                //   14..19 HTYPE/PTYPE/HLEN/PLEN
                //   20..21 OPER
                //   22..27 SHA (sender MAC)   <- frame + 14 + 8
                //   28..31 SPA (sender IPv4)  <- frame + 14 + 14
                val sender_ip = ipv4(
                    int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 16]) & 0xff,
                    int(frame[14 + 17]) & 0xff)
                inet_arp_insert(sender_ip, frame + 14 + 8)
                // Keep the boot-demo ICMP echo on the first gateway
                // reply so the existing pcap regression still shows
                // the full ARP → ICMP round-trip.
                if pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 14, INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B, INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D) != 0
                    inet_send_icmp_echo(0x1234, 1)
                return 1
            return 0
        if etype == ETHERTYPE_IPV4
            if len < 14 + 20
                return 0
            val ihl_bytes = (int(frame[14]) & 0x0f) * 4
            if ihl_bytes < 20
                return 0
            // Accept unicast to us OR the limited-broadcast
            // 255.255.255.255. The broadcast allowance lets DHCP
            // OFFER/ACK through pre-lease (we have no IP yet at
            // DISCOVER time, so the server replies by broadcast
            // per RFC 2131 §4.1).
            val is_unicast = pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, INET_OUR_IP_A, INET_OUR_IP_B, INET_OUR_IP_C, INET_OUR_IP_D)
            val is_bcast = pkt_match_ipv4(frame, 14 + 16, 255, 255, 255, 255)
            if is_unicast == 0
                if is_bcast == 0
                    return 0
            val proto = int(frame[14 + 9]) & 0xff
            if proto == IP_PROTO_ICMP
                val icmp_off = 14 + ihl_bytes
                if len < icmp_off + 8
                    return 0
                val itype = int(frame[icmp_off]) & 0xff
                if itype == ICMP_ECHO_REQ
                    inet_send_icmp_reply(frame, len)
                    return 1
                if itype == ICMP_ECHO_REP
                    inet_bump_pings_received()
                    val rep_id = pkt_read_u16(frame, icmp_off + 4)
                    val rep_seq = pkt_read_u16(frame, icmp_off + 6)
                    val rep_src = ipv4(
                        int(frame[14 + 12]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 13]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 14]) & 0xff,
                        int(frame[14 + 15]) & 0xff)
                    inet_ping_deliver(rep_src, rep_id, rep_seq, int(uptime()))
                    return 1
                return 0
            if proto == IP_PROTO_UDP
                inet_handle_udp(frame, len, ihl_bytes)
                return 1
            if proto == IP_PROTO_TCP
                inet_tcp_handle_segment(frame, len, ihl_bytes)
                return 1
            return 0
        0


### RX drain

Called on every `ipc_recv_notify` wake where the result was -2
(a nic notification). Drains the RX queue non-blockingly, feeding
each frame into `inet_handle_frame`.

    inet_drain_rx()
        var frame: [1514]byte
        while true
            val n = inet_nic_recv_packet(frame, 1514)
            if n <= 0
                break
            inet_handle_frame(frame, n)


### Link-layer init

Called once from `inet_server()` after it registers its port.
Fetches our MAC from the nic, subscribes for RX notifications,
and fires the boot ARP request for the gateway (so the familiar
ARP + ICMP echo pcap still drops out of a cold boot).

Returns 1 if the nic responded and we subscribed successfully, 0
if the nic isn't reachable (e.g. x86 builds without virtio-net).
UDP loopback works either way.

    inet_link_init() -> int
        var mac: [6]byte
        if inet_nic_get_mac(mac) != 0
            return 0
        inet_set_our_mac(mac)
        if inet_nic_subscribe_rx() != 0
            return 0
        inet_send_arp_request(INET_GW_IP_A, INET_GW_IP_B, INET_GW_IP_C, INET_GW_IP_D)
        1


### inet_handle_epoll_sub / unsub / inst_close

Wire format for SUB/UNSUB: cmd(1) + kind(1) + fd(2 BE) +
epoll_inst(1) = 5 bytes in. INST_CLOSE: cmd(1) + epoll_inst(1)
= 2 bytes in. Reply is always status(1).

SUB upserts — if the same (kind, fd, owner_tid, epoll_inst)
quad is already registered, it's a no-op success. The shim
calls SUB on every CTL_ADD and CTL_MOD; the `events` mask isn't
shipped because inet's job is just "wake on edge", not filtering.

    inet_handle_epoll_sub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        val inst = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        val existing = inet_epoll_find_sub(kind, fd, sender, inst)
        if existing >= 0
            reply[0] = byte(0)
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        val slot = inet_epoll_alloc_sub()
        if slot < 0
            reply[0] = byte(-28)            // -ENOSPC
            ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
            return
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 1
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].kind = kind
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].inet_fd = fd
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].owner_tid = sender
        inet_epoll_subs[slot].epoll_inst = inst
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_epoll_unsub(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val kind = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        val fd = inet_read_u16(buf, 2) & 0xffff
        val inst = int(buf[4]) & 0xff
        var reply: [1]byte
        val slot = inet_epoll_find_sub(kind, fd, sender, inst)
        if slot >= 0
            inet_epoll_subs[slot].in_use = 0
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)

    inet_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender: int, buf: *byte)
        val inst = int(buf[1]) & 0xff
        inet_epoll_drop_inst(sender, inst)
        var reply: [1]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)


### Server main loop

Uses `ipc_recv_notify` so the same loop wakes on both socket IPC
(SOCKET/BIND/SENDTO/...) and async nic RX notifications. On a
notification we drain the nic's RX queue via
`inet_nic_recv_packet` and dispatch each frame through
`inet_handle_frame` (ARP + ICMP echo).

Before the loop, `inet_link_init()` grabs our MAC from the nic,
subscribes for RX notifications, and fires the boot ARP request
for the gateway. It's a best-effort call — if the nic isn't
reachable (e.g. the x86 build, which has no virtio-net yet) we
silently skip link-layer setup and still serve UDP loopback.

    inet_server()
        inet_init_sockets()
        inet_tcp_init()
        inet_epoll_init()
        inet_port = port_create()
        port_register(inet_port, "inet")
        rs_ready()

        inet_link_init()

        // Periodic wakeup for TCP retransmit / TIME_WAIT scans.
        // 5 ticks ≈ 50 ms on a 100 Hz scheduler timer.
        svc_timer_subscribe(5)

        var buf: [540]byte
        while 1 == 1
            val r = ipc_recv_notify(inet_port, buf, INET_MSG_BUF)
            inet_tcp_scan_timers()
            inet_udp_scan_timeouts()
            if r == -2
                notify_read_self()
                inet_drain_rx()
            else if r >= 0
                val sender = r
                val cmd = int(buf[0]) & 0xff
                cmd match
                    INET_CMD_SOCKET      -> inet_handle_socket(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_BIND        -> inet_handle_bind(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_SENDTO      -> inet_handle_sendto(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_RECVFROM    -> inet_handle_recvfrom(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT -> inet_handle_recvfrom_timeout(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_CLOSE       -> inet_handle_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT -> inet_handle_tcp_connect(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SEND    -> inet_handle_tcp_send(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_RECV    -> inet_handle_tcp_recv(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE   -> inet_handle_tcp_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN -> inet_handle_tcp_shutdown(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN  -> inet_handle_tcp_listen(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT  -> inet_handle_tcp_accept(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_SET_IP_CONFIG -> inet_handle_set_ip_config(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_GET_IP_CONFIG -> inet_handle_get_ip_config(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ARP_PROBE   -> inet_handle_arp_probe(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ICMP_SEND   -> inet_handle_icmp_send(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_ICMP_CHECK  -> inet_handle_icmp_check(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_PID_EXIT    -> inet_handle_pid_exit(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME -> inet_handle_udp_getname(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME -> inet_handle_tcp_getname(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_recv_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_POLL        -> inet_handle_poll(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB   -> inet_handle_epoll_sub(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB -> inet_handle_epoll_unsub(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE -> inet_handle_epoll_inst_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_accept_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB -> inet_handle_tcp_connect_nb(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF  -> inet_handle_tcp_setbuf(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF  -> inet_handle_tcp_getbuf(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE -> inet_handle_tcp_abort_close(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_DEBUG   -> inet_handle_tcp_debug(sender, buf)
                    INET_CMD_TCP_OOO_SELFTEST -> inet_handle_tcp_ooo_selftest(sender, buf)
                    else ->
                        var reply: [1]byte
                        reply[0] = byte(-1)
                        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 1)
